Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Thank you very much Shu Fai Cheung (張樹輝) for your function, and again I thank Christian because I have seen the code you provided on October 11th 2022.

As my model is a CFA, I first looked for solutions in existing functions in lavaan but it is not so straight: parameterestimates() and standardizedSolution() are enough for models like the popular visual-textual-speed because all possible correlations within the model are already in “Parameter Estimates” (default output) but my model is a third-degree CFA and not all possible correlations are in “Parameter Estimates”. Let’s say that, as seen in a plot, two different subfactors may be in two unrelated corners.

lavInspect(results, "cor.lv <http://cor.lv>") provides all possible pairs of correlations. Maybe standardizedSolution() provides all of them by using GLIST, est, and partable arguments but I disregarded this approach after reading the Note “The est, GLIST, and partable arguments are not meant for everyday users, but for authors of external R packages that depend on lavaan. Only to be used with great caution.”

At the end I ran the following, which is easier than your elaborated approaches (for instance, no lav_matrix() is used):

bootCorrelations <- bootstrapLavaan(results, R=2000,FUN=lavInspect, what="cor.lv <http://cor.lv>")

And then the 95% CI will be at quantiles 2.5 and 97.5%

I would appreciate if you could confirm this is also correct. The means of the bootstrap are pretty similar to cor.lv <http://cor.lv> correlations.

Again, thank you very much for your attention. I will appreciate any help.

Best regards,

Xavier Suriol

